# Instructions

## Git Command Restrictions

- Do not run Git commands unless the user specifically asks for a Git operation.
- A request to use Git does not authorize state changes. Before any Git command that
  changes repository state, explain the exact operation and obtain separate, explicit
  confirmation for it.
- State-changing commands include staging, committing, checkout or switch, reset, clean,
  stash, branch creation or deletion, cherry-pick, rebase, merge, and apply.
- Never infer confirmation from a general request, prior approval, or the presence of
  existing changes. If confirmation is not given, do not run the state-changing command.
- Do not run `git diff --check`; it is not useful here and may not reflect the user's changes.

## Required Markdown Lint

- After any Markdown file change, run `npx markdownlint-cli2` on every changed Markdown file.
- Do not substitute another Markdown checker. Report if the required lint command cannot run.

## 🚨 CRITICAL: Markdown Creation Checklist 🚨

**BEFORE creating or editing ANY markdown file, verify EVERY item:**

- [ ] ALL code blocks have language specifiers (\`\`\`python, \`\`\`bash, \`\`\`text) - NEVER use \`\`\` alone
- [ ] ALL lists have blank line BEFORE the first item
- [ ] ALL lists have blank line AFTER the last item
- [ ] Ordered lists numbered sequentially (1, 2, 3, 4) - NO gaps or wrong numbers
- [ ] NO duplicate heading text at the same level
- [ ] Only ONE H1 (\`#\`) per document
- [ ] Line length ≤ 120 characters (break long lines across multiple lines)
- [ ] Tables use compact format: \`| column |\` with spaces around pipes

**During editing, validate only requested files using approved, available tooling:**

```bash
npx markdownlint-cli2 "path/to/file.md"
```

Do not search outside authorized directories or silently install tools. If npx is unavailable or fails, state
that Markdown lint was not run. Apply automatic fixes using `--fix`

**Common mistakes that cause 90% of linting errors:**

1. Forgetting blank line before list (text immediately followed by \`-\` or \`1.\`)
2. Forgetting blank line after list (list immediately followed by text)
3. Duplicate heading names (add qualifiers or use subheadings)
4. Code blocks without language (use \`\`\`text if unsure)
